  nodePositions: []
  nodeAbstract: 
  nodeTextLayers:
  - title: 'What is the membrane and what is it made of?



'
    basic: "Every cell is enclosed by a membrane that contains large biomolecules.
      \nThe membrane is primarily made up of two layers of phospholipid (fat) called
      the phospholipid bilayer."
    intermediate: 
    advanced: "The current model of the plasma membrane is a fluid lipid surface
      integrated with carbohydrates, proteins and glycoproteins. \nThese integrated
      biomolecules are functional components of the cell and can move freely along
      the membrane."
  - title: 'What is the lipid bilayer and why is it important?



'
    basic: "The phospholipid bilayer is two layers of phospholipid molecules arranged
      with opposing ends facing each other. \nThe lipid bilayer separates the inside
      from the outside of the cell, allowing the cell to carry out necessary functions."
    intermediate: 
    advanced: "The hydrophobic lipid bilayer forms a selective barrier preventing
      diffusion of polar and charged molecules. \nTransporters, receptors and enzymes
      residing on the membrane carry out cellular functions such as communication,
      material exchange and recognition."
